  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 402 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nUpper flow will become generally zonal by midweek across most of \nCONUS. The departing frontal boundary from Monday/Tuesday will not \nonly bring a slight cooldown, with highs forecasted to be in the low \nto mid 80s across the region but will also prevent the northward \nexpansion of richer low-level moisture for the next few days. This \nwill coincide with high pressure settling into the region, \nprecluding any high chances for measureable precipitation during\nthis time frame. A break from the zonal flow in way of an upper\nlevel ridge is forecasted to trek across the northern Plains \nThursday/Friday. This ridge is expected to further build upon \nthe high pressure that is settled into the region and bringing \ndaytime highs up to mid to upper 80s Thursday and the upper 80s \nto low 90s Friday. Additionally, dry air is expected to creep in\nfrom the west with humidity dropping to the low to mid teens \nalong and west of HWY 61. This area of low humidity will have \nsome overlap with breezy winds of 15 to 20 mph gusting up to \nnear 30 mph out of the southwest Thursday, then out of the \nnorthwest Friday to highlight at least the potential for \nelevated fire weather conditions, especially with a lot of areas\nstill being in D2 drought. \n\nMoving into the weekend, deep troughing will move onshore into the \nPacific Northwest sometime early Saturday. This feature will \ncontinue to dig southward and move across the Rockies into the \nnorthern/central Plains through the weekend. In the lead up to this, \nweak southwesterly flow will establish itself across Nebraska. This \nwill allow for better moisture transport to again reach our neck of \nthe woods. HREF guidance has forecasted PWATs of a range of 1.0 to \n1.25 inches which would put us near the 95th percentile with other \nmodels pushing that percentile to near 140% above normal. This would \nsuggest the potential for a greater rain potential to go along with \na cooldown. While high PWATs are forecasted, guidance has still been \na bit bullish on total accumulations during this period, maintaining \nonly a 30 to 50 percent chance of the region seeing at least 0.10\" \nof rainfall each day Saturday through early Monday morning. The \nhigher end percent of this is located on the east side of our CWA \nfrom HWY 83 and east.",
